From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 20:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Let windows hide from screen captures

An app that records or streams the display it is on captures itself: a
streaming app showing its own monitor fills the stream with copies of its
preview. A recorder's controls have the same problem. Nothing let a client
keep a window on the screen but out of what is captured.

clutter_actor_set_hidden_from_capture() makes an actor, with its effects
and children, skip paints that carry CLUTTER_PAINT_FLAG_CAPTURE, which
every paint of the stage outside its views has. A ClutterClone of the
actor, or of one of its descendants, skips them too. An offscreen effect
draws again for a capture while any actor is hidden, and for the screen
after it, so an image it cached during a screen paint (a preview holding a
clone of a hidden window) never reaches a capture. Painting the actor
alone, which window streams and window screenshots do, is unaffected.

meta_window_set_hidden_from_capture() and MetaWindow:hidden-from-capture
set it on the window actor. A window transient for a hidden window (a
dialog, a menu, a popup) is hidden with it. Wayland clients ask through
luma_window_capture_v1: get_window_capture(wl_surface), then a
double-buffered set_visibility(visible|hidden) that reaches the window on
the xdg_toplevel's next commit; destroying the object shows the window
again. X11 clients set the _LUMA_WINDOW_HIDDEN_FROM_CAPTURE CARDINAL
property to 1. Hiding only removes content from captures, so no
permission is involved.

Monitor streams keep copying the screen (the scanout buffer or a blit of
the view) unless meta_compositor_is_capture_hidden_in() says a hidden
window is mapped on that monitor or shown through a mapped clone
anywhere; then they paint the stage, as they do for monitors spanning
several outputs. Without a hidden window the question is a counter check.
Area streams and screenshots already paint the stage.
